---
// /for-orgs/nominate/ - a hiker putting a club on the map for them (#1539).
//
// THE HIKER IS NOT SPEAKING FOR THE ORGANIZATION AND MUST NOT BE ASKED TO.
// They know their local club has trails and roughly where to find them; that
// is the whole of what this collects, and it is why the form is one field.
// Everything after that field is us doing the work, and the page says so.
//
// WHAT "WE GO LOOKING" HONESTLY MEANS, and the page must not overstate it.
// Nothing here probes anything in the browser. Submitting records an
// unclaimed organization (`POST /clubs/nominations`) and a person reads it -
// which is the true state of SOURCE_REGISTRY.md today, where all 33 sources
// across nine organizations arrived because a maintainer went looking and
// read terms by hand. The discovery panel below is drawn as an EXAMPLE of
// what comes back, labelled as one, rather than as a live result: a fake
// result presented as real would be this page lying on its first screen.
//
// The flat-file refusal is stated here and not only at the API, because the
// person reading this is about to go and ask a club for something. "Ask them
// for the layer behind it" is the sentence that makes that ask possible.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import OrgNav from '../../components/OrgNav.astro'

const title = 'OurHike — Put a club on the map for them'
const description =
  "Hiking somewhere new and the local club publishes maps nobody can use offline? Give us their website. You submit their trails; they take it over whenever they are ready."
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <OrgNav />
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <span class="org-eyebrow">ANY SIGNED-IN HIKER CAN DO THIS</span>
      <h1 class="org-form__title">Put a club on the map for them</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Hiking somewhere new and the local club publishes maps nobody can use
        offline? Give us their website. We go looking for their trail data, and
        their trails go on the map — <strong>unclaimed</strong>, until somebody
        from the org comes and takes it over.
      </p>

      <form class="org-form" id="nominate-form" novalidate style="margin-top: 24px">
        <fieldset class="org-form__group">
          <span class="org-eyebrow">STEP 1 · THEIR WEBSITE</span>
          {
            /* NO NAME FIELD, on the maintainer's decision of 2026-09-17. The
               design opens with the website and nothing else, and asking for
               the name first made a hiker answer the question the reading is
               there to answer. */
          }
          <label class="org-label" for="nominate-website">Club website</label>
          <input
            class="org-input"
            id="nominate-website"
            name="website"
            type="url"
            inputmode="url"
            placeholder="https://carolinamountainclub.org"
          />
          <p class="org-help">
            That is all we need. We read the public site the way you would —
            looking for a maps page, a GIS server, a downloads link, or a data
            portal.
          </p>
          {
            /* WHO DOES THE READING, said before the button rather than after.
               The address goes to a model at Anthropic's API, which is a
               third party and is not us. An organization gets asked this
               question properly - `Club.assist_opted_in`, off until one of
               their admins turns it on - and nobody can be asked here,
               because the organization this is about does not exist in our
               database yet and a hiker cannot agree on their behalf. So what
               is owed on this page is the plain sentence, and the smallest
               thing sent that could do the job: one public web address, and
               nothing about the person typing it. */
          }
          <p class="org-help">
            The address goes to a model at Anthropic's API to do the reading —
            just the address, nothing about you. Skip this and fill the form in
            yourself if you would rather it did not.
          </p>
          <div class="org-inline" style="margin-top: 10px">
            <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="nominate-look">
              Have a look at their site
            </button>
            <span class="org-help" id="nominate-look-note"></span>
          </div>
          {
            /* WHAT THIS PANEL IS, and the sentence it has to keep. It reports
               what a model could see on a public page. It does not verify
               anything, it is not a decision, and the organization it is
               about has not been contacted - which is exactly what the
               heading below says, because a nomination that arrived reading
               like an endorsement would be worse than none. */
          }
          <div class="org-found" id="nominate-found" hidden>
            <span class="org-eyebrow">WHAT WE COULD SEE ON THEIR SITE</span>
            <p class="org-help" id="nominate-found-text"></p>
            <p class="org-help">
              <strong>Read, not verified.</strong> Nobody at the club has been
              contacted and nothing here is a commitment — yours or theirs. Add
              anything it missed below; most orgs keep their data in more than
              one place.
            </p>
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        {
          /* THE OPTIONAL BLOCK IS GONE, on the maintainer's decision of
             2026-09-17: a data link and a free-text note the design does not
             ask for. A hiker who knows where an organization's FeatureServer
             lives is not the hiker this form is for, and the reading finds
             the layer anyway. */
        }

        <button class="btn org-form__submit" type="submit">Submit their trails</button>
        <p class="org-help org-help--centred">
          This gives you no standing at their org, and asks nothing of them.
        </p>
      </form>

      <div class="org-found" style="margin-top: 36px">
        <span class="org-eyebrow">WHAT COMES BACK · AN EXAMPLE, NOT A LIVE RESULT</span>
        <h2 class="org-found__title">What we look for on a club's site</h2>
        <p class="org-help">
          Every one of these is checked by a person before anything of a club's
          reaches a map. This is what that reading usually turns up.
        </p>
        <ul class="org-sources">
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="usable">
            <span class="org-source__what">An ArcGIS FeatureServer</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">usable</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >Line features with a blaze field and a published edit date. The
              best case: we can read it again tomorrow and tell them what
              changed.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="usable">
            <span class="org-source__what">A WFS or GeoJSON endpoint</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">usable</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >Same test, different shape. Anything at a URL that answers with
              geometry and keeps answering.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="closures">
            <span class="org-source__what">A closures or alerts feed</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">closures</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >Live JSON we can read hourly once they approve it — the one
              source where being an hour old matters.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="refused">
            <span class="org-source__what">PDF trail maps</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">not accepted</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >We do not take these. <strong
                >A PDF cannot be re-read, so it is stale the day it is
                uploaded</strong
              > — and taking a snapshot while implying a feed is worse than taking
              nothing. Ask them for the layer behind it.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="found">
            <span class="org-source__what">Membership and donation pages</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">found</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >We put these in front of every hiker walking their trails. The
              money goes straight to them and is never routed through
              OurHike.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="org-source" data-verdict="licence">
            <span class="org-source__what">Whatever their terms say</span>
            <span class="org-source__verdict">read by a person</span>
            <span class="org-source__why"
              >Asked <em>before</em> the bytes are in a build, which is the one
              thing this project has got wrong twice already.</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="org-note" style="margin-top: 28px">
        <p>
          <strong>Nothing publishes because you submitted it.</strong> A
          nomination records the organization as unclaimed and puts it in front
          of a person here. Three admins <em>at that organization</em> still have
          to approve before a single section of theirs reaches a phone — the same
          rule as an org that signs itself up, and the reason submitting
          somebody else's trails is a favour rather than a liability.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script src="../../scripts/nominateLook.js"></script>
</Base>
